<DocsLayout
  title="Architecture"
  subtitle="Source-oriented map of the nikcli monorepo, CLI lifecycle, server stack, session runtime, storage model, integrations, mobile host, and SDK boundary."
  description="Source-oriented architecture map of nikcli’s monorepo, runtime boundaries, server stack, mobile host, and SDK integration."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      nikcli is a Bun monorepo centered on <code>packages/nikcli</code>. The CLI process initializes
      global paths and logging, resolves project instances, loads config through Effect layers, and
      then runs one of several front doors: the OpenTUI terminal app, one-shot commands, an HTTP
      server, workspace hosting, mobile hosting, companion UI, remote control, or automation flows.
      Most of those surfaces converge on the same session, provider, tool, permission, storage, and
      event services.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Single runtime core" eyebrow="packages/nikcli">
        <p>CLI, TUI, server routes, mobile host, remote control, providers, tools, permissions, and storage live in the main package.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Generated client" eyebrow="packages/sdk">
        <p>The web, TUI, mobile, and external clients use the SDK boundary instead of importing server internals.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Multiple shells" eyebrow="TUI, HTTP, mobile, companion">
        <p>Different user surfaces route into the same Hono server app or session services.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Effect services" eyebrow="Runtime state">
        <p>Config, project instance, sessions, storage, and provider services are assembled through Effect layers and per-instance context.</p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Read architecture from runtime edges">
      <p>
        The fastest way to orient is to start at <code>packages/nikcli/src/index.ts</code>, then follow
        the command into <code>server/server.ts</code>, <code>cli/cmd/tui/thread.ts</code>,
        <code>session/index.ts</code>, or the command-specific module. Those files reveal which shared
        services are actually on the hot path.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="package-map" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Package map</h2>
    <p>
      The repository is a Bun workspace. The most important boundary is that
      <code>packages/nikcli</code> owns the executable runtime, while sibling packages provide clients,
      UI shells, plugins, remote services, and companion surfaces.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Main CLI" eyebrow="packages/nikcli">
        <p>Command registry, TUI, server app, session engine, provider registry, tools, permissions, config, storage, connectors, MCP, LSP, mobile host, and remote control.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="SDK" eyebrow="packages/sdk">
        <p>Generated JavaScript client and schemas for the server API. Regenerate it after changing server endpoints in <code>server.ts</code>.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Studio" eyebrow="packages/studio">
        <p>Desktop UI package that consumes nikcli APIs rather than owning session/runtime internals.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Plugin system" eyebrow="packages/plugin">
        <p>Reusable plugin API surface and package-level plugin tooling.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Remote" eyebrow="packages/remote">
        <p>Remote execution and sharing support used by remote control workflows.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Companion" eyebrow="packages/companion">
        <p>Companion services and browser-facing workflows that connect to the nikcli server surface.</p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
    <DocCode language="bash">
bun install
bun run typecheck
bun test
bun run --cwd packages/nikcli dev
./packages/sdk/js/script/build.ts
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="cli-lifecycle" class="doc-section">
    <h2>CLI lifecycle</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/index.ts</code> is the executable entrypoint. It builds the yargs CLI,
      installs global error handlers, initializes global paths, initializes logging, sets
      <code>AGENT=1</code> and <code>NIKCLI=1</code>, registers commands, and finally forces process
      exit to avoid hanging subprocesses.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Global initialization</strong> creates data, config, cache, state, log, bin, and repo paths.</li>
      <li><strong>Logging setup</strong> respects <code>--print-logs</code>, <code>--log-level</code>, and local development defaults.</li>
      <li><strong>Command registration</strong> wires ACP, MCP, ads, TUI, run, goal, generate, debug, auth, account, agent, upgrade, quickstart, doctor, serve, workspace, web, heap, models, locale, stats, export/import, GitHub, PR, session, image, speech, remote, companion, mobile, routine, usage, mission, loop, and plugin commands. <code>--attach</code> is a flag on <code>run</code>, not a separate command.</li>
      <li><strong>Failure formatting</strong> uses <code>FormatError</code> for known errors and points unknown errors at the log file.</li>
      <li><strong>Process exit</strong> runs in <code>finally</code> to avoid hanging child processes, especially external MCP servers.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="The default command is the TUI">
      <p>
        Because <code>TuiThreadCommand</code> is registered as <code>$0 [project]</code>, a bare
        <code>nikcli</code> command starts the TUI. One-shot agent execution goes through
        <code>nikcli run</code>.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="instance-model" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Instance model</h2>
    <p>
      Runtime services are scoped to project instances rather than process globals wherever possible.
      Commands and server routes run inside an instance context that carries the active directory,
      worktree, config paths, and service layers. This is why the TUI worker can host a server,
      respond to SDK calls, and still dispose project state cleanly through
      <code>Instance.disposeAll()</code>.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Responsibility</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>global</code></td><td>Cross-project filesystem roots, cache versioning, data directories, log directories, and managed binary paths.</td><td>Every process needs the same durable root model before config or storage can load.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>project/instance</code></td><td>Current directory, worktree, lifecycle, and per-project state.</td><td>Lets TUI, server, mobile, and workspace requests isolate project behavior.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Config.defaultLayer</code></td><td>Remote, global, project, env, content, directory, mode, provider, command, agent, and plugin config merging.</td><td>Prevents commands from manually reimplementing config precedence.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Storage.defaultLayer</code></td><td>JSON storage, migrations, locks, cache, transactions, and typed missing-file errors.</td><td>Unifies persistence for CLI, TUI, server, mobile, sessions, goals, and stats.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Session.defaultLayer</code></td><td>Session lookup, creation, updates, messages, tool parts, goals, todos, summaries, and processing.</td><td>Session behavior stays shared across local and HTTP surfaces.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="server-stack" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Server stack</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/server/server.ts</code> builds the Hono app and request order. Public
      share routes are registered before user auth. Then server auth, request logging, CORS, global
      metadata, workspace context, docs, optional experimental HTTP API bridge, and classic routes are
      installed.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Public shares</td><td>Routes intended for shared access are mounted before authenticated user middleware.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>User auth</td><td>User/session middleware applies to authenticated app surfaces.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Server auth</td><td>Bearer, Tailscale, and Basic Auth modes can protect the server.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Logging and CORS</td><td>Requests are logged and CORS is applied according to server options.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/global</code></td><td>Exposes global server metadata used by SDK clients and UI surfaces.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workspace context</td><td>Directory and workspace-scoped requests enter the correct instance context.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>/doc</code></td><td>Serves API docs/schema surfaces.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HTTP API bridge</td><td>Mounted when <code>NIKCLI_EXPERIMENTAL_HTTPAPI</code> is enabled.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Classic routes</td><td>Session, event, TUI, mobile, connector, MCP, analytics, workspace, chat, and related route groups.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="session-runtime" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Session runtime</h2>
    <p>
      Sessions are the main execution unit. The session layer persists metadata, messages, message
      parts, diffs, summaries, todos, goals, and share state; coordinates agent execution; publishes
      events; and exposes both SDK routes and internal service calls. The one-shot command, TUI,
      mobile host, remote control, workspace hosting, and server API all converge here.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Concern</th><th>Primary modules</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Session metadata</td><td><code>session/index.ts</code></td><td>Create, update, delete, list, title, plan path, share lookup, and project-scoped session keys.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Messages and parts</td><td><code>session/message-v2.ts</code>, processors</td><td>Message records and part records are normalized in JSON storage.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LLM request loop</td><td><code>session/llm.ts</code>, <code>session/llm/native-runtime.ts</code></td><td>Normalizes prompts, selects provider/runtime, streams responses, handles tool calls, and repairs prompt-shape issues.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Goals and todos</td><td><code>session/goal</code>, todo services</td><td>Persist planning state and task state alongside the transcript.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sharing and remote</td><td>share, remote, server routes</td><td>Expose sessions to external clients while preserving session storage as the source of truth.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="llm-providers" class="doc-section">
    <h2>LLM and providers</h2>
    <p>
      Provider configuration is split from the session hot path. <code>provider/provider.ts</code>
      builds provider and model metadata from bundled SDK adapters, models.dev, config providers,
      saved auth, dynamic providers such as Ollama and nikcli inference, and plugin-supplied auth or
      model loaders. <code>session/llm.ts</code> is where requests are actually shaped and sent.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Responsibility</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Provider registry</td><td>Discovers providers, applies enabled/disabled filters, merges config, loads auth, filters deprecated or alpha models, and caches results.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Model selection</td><td>Resolves provider/model ids from config, CLI args, dialogs, or session state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Request shaping</td><td>Converts stored messages into model-compatible message arrays and handles provider-specific prompt constraints.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Native runtime</td><td>Uses <code>@nikcli-ai/llm</code> for supported providers when the native runtime path is available.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refresh</td><td><code>Provider.refresh()</code> clears and rebuilds caches after auth or config changes.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="tools-permissions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Tools and permissions</h2>
    <p>
      Tools are available to agents through the tool registry and are guarded by the permission
      system where needed. Permissions are evaluated from rules, path patterns, and action defaults,
      with pending prompts published as bus events and persisted always-approvals stored under the
      project storage namespace. See
      <a href="/docs/permissions#modes">Permission modes</a> for the TUI presets
      (<code>require_approval</code> / <code>approve_for_me</code> / <code>full_access</code> /
      <code>custom</code>) that write the per-agent permission map.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Tool registry</td><td>Registers filesystem, shell, edit, search, web, MCP, LSP, and domain-specific tools according to config and feature flags.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Permission evaluation</td><td>Finds the last matching allow, deny, or ask rule, expands home paths, and resolves action-specific policy.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Interactive asks</td><td>Publishes pending permission requests and waits for a one-time, always, or reject reply.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Persistence</td><td>Always approvals are stored as project-scoped records in the JSON storage layer under <code>permission/:projectID</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LSP tool gate</td><td>The agent-facing <code>lsp</code> tool is available only when the experimental LSP tool flag is enabled.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mode presets</td><td>The TUI exposes three presets that write the agent's <code>config.agent.&lt;name&gt;.permission</code> map. The default is <code>approve_for_me</code> (allow trusted local work, ask for shell/web/external/sensitive reads).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="storage-state" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Storage and state</h2>
    <p>
      Durable state uses two storage families. The JSON object store under
      <code>Global.Path.data/storage</code> owns high-level runtime records. SQLite databases under
      the data root own indexed tables such as users, accounts, mobile tokens, and workspaces.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Primary data</th><th>Why this shape</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>JSON object store</td><td>Projects, sessions, messages, parts, diffs, goals, todos, routines, permissions, and share metadata.</td><td>Simple file-level records, migrations, locks, cache, and easy inspection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>users.db</code></td><td>Local users, sessions, chat contacts, and chat messages.</td><td>Relational lookup and constraints for account-like data.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>accounts.db</code></td><td>Nikcli account records and tokens.</td><td>Credential and active account state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>mobile_auth.db</code></td><td>Hashed mobile bearer tokens, expiry, and last-used timestamps.</td><td>Fast token validation and revocation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>workspaces.db</code></td><td>Workspace records, target config, status, and events.</td><td>Workspace event history and status queries.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="events-sync" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Events and sync</h2>
    <p>
      The server publishes typed events through bus definitions and exposes event streams to SDK
      clients. The TUI consumes them through <code>SDKProvider</code>, batches deliveries, and updates
      Solid stores. Mobile and companion surfaces consume the same event model over server routes.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Runtime module changes state</strong>, such as session update, message part append, permission request, or TUI command publish.</li>
      <li><strong>Bus event is published</strong> with a typed schema and payload.</li>
      <li><strong>Server event route streams it</strong> to SDK subscribers.</li>
      <li><strong>Client sync provider handles it</strong> and updates route, transcript, provider, permission, toast, or workspace state.</li>
      <li><strong>UI renders from synchronized state</strong> instead of manually polling each route.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section id="integrations" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Integrations</h2>
    <p>
      nikcli has three integration families: MCP servers, first-party connector registry entries, and
      plugins. MCP extends tool/resource/prompt surfaces. Connectors integrate named services and bot
      transports. Plugins can contribute auth, providers, commands, server hooks, and TUI routes.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Integration</th><th>Source modules</th><th>Runtime role</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>MCP</td><td><code>mcp/index.ts</code>, <code>mcp/auth.ts</code>, HTTP API routes</td><td>Local stdio and remote Streamable HTTP/SSE MCP servers, OAuth, tools, prompts, and resources.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Connectors</td><td><code>connectors/registry.ts</code>, <code>connectors/auth.ts</code>, <code>connectors/credentials.ts</code></td><td>Figma, Slack, GitHub, Lovable, and bot connector metadata, operations, auth, and health checks.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Chatbots</td><td><code>server/routes/chatbot.ts</code>, chatbot command modules</td><td>Discord, Slack, Teams, Google Chat, Linear, and GitHub webhook entrypoints.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plugins</td><td><code>plugin/index.ts</code>, <code>plugin/install.ts</code>, <code>cli/cmd/plug.ts</code></td><td>Internal plugins, config plugins, installable package plugins, server exports, and TUI exports.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LSP</td><td><code>lsp/index.ts</code>, <code>lsp/server.ts</code>, <code>tool/lsp.ts</code></td><td>Optional language-server clients and an experimental agent-facing code-intelligence tool.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="mobile-remote" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Mobile and remote</h2>
    <p>
      Mobile hosting is a server route family, not the same feature as terminal remote control. The
      mobile host serves <code>/mobile/*</code> routes, validates bearer tokens from
      <code>mobile_auth.db</code>, streams sessions over SSE, handles permission replies, exposes
      routine and repository workflows, and remains compatible with hosted environments such as
      Railway when using the host path.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Entry</th><th>Architecture note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>TUI remote control</td><td><code>remote</code> command and TUI slash commands</td><td>Controls a running terminal session through server/TUI events and shared URLs.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Run against a server</td><td><code>nikcli run --attach &lt;url&gt;</code></td><td>One-shot agent execution attached to an existing nikcli server (no separate <code>attach</code> command).</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile host</td><td><code>mobile</code> command and <code>server/routes/mobile.ts</code></td><td>Hosts mobile app APIs, auth tokens, sessions, routines, GitHub-backed repos, notifications, and Live Activity flows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Companion</td><td><code>companion</code> command</td><td>Desktop browser companion surface using the server and SDK event model.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Workspace server</td><td><code>workspace-serve</code> and workspace routes</td><td>Hosts workspace contexts, status, events, and session proxying.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Good mobile smoke test">
      <p>
        <code>POST /mobile/auth/token</code> is a better first smoke than a deep bootstrap call
        because it proves route mounting and auth/token plumbing before requiring a full mobile
        session bootstrap.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="sdk-boundary" class="doc-section">
    <h2>SDK boundary</h2>
    <p>
      The generated JavaScript SDK is the contract between server routes and clients. The TUI imports
      <code>@nikcli-ai/sdk/v2</code> even when running in-process; it swaps only the fetch/event
      transport. Web, mobile, companion, and external clients use the same conceptual surface.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Server change</th><th>Follow-up</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Change an endpoint in <code>packages/nikcli/src/server/server.ts</code></td><td>Regenerate the JavaScript SDK with <code>./packages/sdk/js/script/build.ts</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Add or change typed HTTP API routes</td><td>Verify route schemas, generated client methods, and any TUI/mobile callers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Change event payloads</td><td>Update server event schema and every sync consumer that narrows on that event type.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Change auth behavior</td><td>Verify direct worker fetch, HTTP SDK clients, attach, mobile, companion, and remote control separately.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="operational-map" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Operational map</h2>
    <p>Use this map to decide where to start for common architecture questions.</p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Start here</th><th>Then inspect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Why does bare <code>nikcli</code> open the terminal app?</td><td><code>src/index.ts</code></td><td><code>cli/cmd/tui/thread.ts</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Which route handles this API call?</td><td><code>server/server.ts</code></td><td>Route group under <code>server/routes</code> or <code>server/httpapi</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Why is config different in a worktree?</td><td><code>config/config.ts</code></td><td><code>config/paths.ts</code> and environment flags</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Why did a provider/model disappear?</td><td><code>provider/provider.ts</code></td><td>Saved auth, config provider entries, enabled/disabled filters, plugin auth/model hooks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where is session content stored?</td><td><code>storage/storage.ts</code></td><td><code>session/index.ts</code>, <code>session/message-v2.ts</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Why is a tool blocked?</td><td><code>permission/next.ts</code></td><td><code>permission/schema.ts</code>, <code>permission/evaluate.ts</code>, the active permission mode (see <a href="/docs/permissions#modes">Permission modes</a>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Why does mobile auth fail?</td><td><code>server/routes/mobile.ts</code></td><td><code>mobile/auth.ts</code> and <code>mobile_auth.db</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Why does the TUI not receive updates?</td><td><code>cli/cmd/tui/context/sdk.tsx</code></td><td><code>cli/cmd/tui/worker.ts</code>, event route, server auth</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="source-anchors" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Source anchors</h2>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>File or directory</th><th>What to inspect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/index.ts</code></td><td>CLI entrypoint, global middleware, command registration order, failure formatting, and forced exit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/global/index.ts</code></td><td>Global path model, data/cache/config/state/log/bin/repos roots, environment overrides, and initialization.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/project/instance.ts</code></td><td>Per-directory instance lifecycle and state scoping.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/effect.ts</code></td><td>Effect helpers, current instance context, and service layer execution helpers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/config/config.ts</code></td><td>Main config merge order, env overrides, mode migration, provider/plugin/command/agent loading, and flag overrides.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/server/server.ts</code></td><td>Hono app construction, middleware order, auth, global route, docs, HTTP API bridge, and classic route mounting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/session/index.ts</code></td><td>Session service, metadata persistence, updates, deletion, share lookup, and plan path selection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/session/llm.ts</code></td><td>Runtime LLM request path, prompt normalization, provider selection, streaming, and tool call loop.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/provider/provider.ts</code></td><td>Provider registry, models.dev integration, config providers, saved auth, plugins, dynamic providers, and refresh.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/tool</code></td><td>Agent-facing tools, tool registration, and domain-specific tools.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>packages/nikcli/src/permission</code></td><td>Permission schemas, evaluation, persisted approvals, and interactive request flow.</td></tr>
